vironment", "Expert ratings cluster at Lean R (7 of 10), with two Tossup calls", "Killin's strong fundraising keeps the race competitive", "Thin, dated, Dem-sponsored polling limits precision"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a modest Republican edge: an R+5 seat, an incumbent running in a decent GOP environment, and a rating consensus of Lean R. Jessica Killin has outraised Jeff Crank and a July Democratic poll showed a one-point race, but the newest polling is old and partisan-sponsored, so the evidence supports only a small shift from the combined estimate.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a shift in the national political environment could move this toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 71 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Crank (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 5th District, anchored in Colorado Springs, leans Republican by about five points, and first-term incumbent Jeff Crank is seeking reelection against Democrat Jessica Killin. Most professional raters call the race Lean Republican, though two rate it a Tossup, reflecting genuine competitiveness. Prediction markets give Crank roughly a two-in-three chance of winning.\n\nKillin has run a well-funded campaign, out-raising Crank and keeping cash on hand roughly even, and a July poll sponsored by Democratic-aligned pollsters showed a one-point race. But that poll is dated and the polling record is thin, so the race rests heavily on fundamentals. The incumbent's edge in a Republican-leaning district in a favorable environment makes him modestly favored, with Killin's fundraising the main source of upside.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.332, "margin": -2.57}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3239, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3532, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.346, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2907, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-2